[
  {
    "role": "paragraph",
    "segment": "Technical quality focuses on the product, its processes, and how it performs in a technical sense, while business quality looks at the products and services from the perspective of business value and objectives.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "When the focus of quality assessment is on products and processes, we usually measure quality with mathematical expressions involving faults, failures, and timing.",
        "img_id": null
      },
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "Rarely is the scope broadened to include a business perspective, where quality is viewed in terms of the products and services being provided by the business in which the software is embedded. That is, we look at the technical value of our products, rather than more broadly at their business value.",
        "img_id": null
      }
    ],
    "type": null
  },
  {
    "role": "paragraph",
    "segment": "Technical quality is evaluated by how the product behaves under technical metrics (like faults, failures, timing) and by attributes such as reliability, maintainability, and performance, as they relate to the product and its development process.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "The quality of the product, the quality of the process that results in the product, and the quality of the product in the context of the business environment in which the product will be used.",
        "img_id": null
      }
    ],
    "type": null
  },
  {
    "role": "paragraph",
    "segment": "Business quality, by contrast, ties quality to what the customer is willing to pay and to the overall business value, enabling trade-offs between cost and quality and helping manage conflicts when they arise.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "By equating quality to what the customer is willing to pay, we can look at trade-offs between cost and quality, and we can manage conflicts when they arise.",
        "img_id": null
      }
    ],
    "type": null
  },
  {
    "role": "paragraph",
    "segment": "Why each is important: technical quality is essential to ensure the product functions correctly, reliably, and securely, and to optimize the development process; business quality is essential to ensure the product delivers measurable value for the organization and its customers and to justify investment through business outcomes.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "In the 1990s, there was a well-publicized focus on process modeling and process improvement in software engineering. Inspired by the work of Deming and Juran, and implemented by companies such as IBM, process guidelines such as the Capability Maturity Model (CMM), ISO 9000, and Software Process Improvement and Capability dEtermination (SPICE) suggested that by improving the software development process, we can improve the quality of the resulting products.",
        "img_id": null
      },
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "The issue of business versus technical value is near and dear to our heart ... If there is not clear (expected) business value (expressed quantitatively: number of claims processed, etc.) then we can't launch a systems project.",
        "img_id": null
      }
    ],
    "type": null
  }
]

