[
  {
    "role": "paragraph",
    "segment": "To represent a morning routine for class or work, use a UML activity diagram to capture the flow of procedures or activities. Start by placing a start node, add each morning activity as rectangular nodes, and connect them with arrows to show the transitions. If there are choices (e.g., first you shower or dress, then pack your bag), insert a decision node to represent the branching between activities. End with an end node when you complete the routine.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "We can use activity diagrams to model the flow of procedures or activities in a class.",
        "img_id": null
      },
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "Figure 6.27 illustrates the notation used in a UML activity diagram. As with state diagrams, the start node is represented by a black dot, and the end node is a smaller black dot inside a white dot.A rectangle represents an activity, with an arrow showing transitions from one activity to another.",
        "img_id": null
      }
    ],
    "type": null
  }
]

