[
  {
    "role": "paragraph",
    "segment": "Organizational structure should fit the level of certainty or uncertainty in the project. Projects with high certainty and repetition are best served by a hierarchical organizational structure, such as a chief programmer team, which stresses rules, specialization, formality, and a clear definition of organizational hierarchy. In contrast, when there is a lot of uncertainty, a more democratic approach with participation in decision making, a loosely defined hierarchy, and open communication can be effective.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "a hierarchical organizational structure  such  as  the  chief  programmer team.These projects require little communication among project members, so they are well-suited to an organization that stresses rules, specialization, formality, and a clear definition of organizational hierarchy.",
        "img_id": null
      },
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "on the other hand, when there is much uncertainty involved in a project, a more democratic approach may be better. For example, if the requirements may change as development proceeds, the project has a degree of uncertainty. Likewise, suppose your customer is building a new piece of hardware to interface with a system; if the exact specification of the hardware is not yet known, then the level of uncertainty is high. Here, participation in decision making, a loosely defined hierarchy, and the encouragement of open communication can be effective.",
        "img_id": null
      }
    ],
    "type": null
  },
  {
    "role": "paragraph",
    "segment": "Understanding work styles helps you be flexible with team members and customers. Work styles indicate priorities, so you can tailor information to what others deem important; for example, an introverted customer may prefer gathering information and a presentation can focus on structure and progress, while an extroverted customer may benefit from questions to surface needs. Work styles also influence who is chosen for a given task, such as intuitive employees preferring design and development, whereas others may suit maintenance programming and design.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "Understanding work styles can help you to be flexible in your approach to other project team members and to customers and users. In particular, work styles give you information about the priorities of others. If a colleague's priorities and interests are different from yours, you can present information to her in terms of what she deems important. For example, suppose Claude is your customer and you are preparing a presentation for him on the status of the project. If Claude is an introvert, you know that he prefers gathering information to giving it.Thus, you may organize your presentation so that it tells him a great deal about how the project is structured and how it is progressing. However, if Claude is an extrovert, you can include questions to allow him to tell you what he wants or needs. Similarly, if Claude is intuitive, you can take advantage of his creativity by soliciting new ideas from him; if he is rational, your presentation can include facts or figures rather than judgments or feelings. Thus, work styles affect interactions among customers, developers, and users.",
        "img_id": null
      },
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "Work styles can also involve choice of worker for a given task.For instance,intuitive employees may prefer design and development (requiring new ideas) to maintenance programming and design (requiring attention to detail and analysis of complex results).",
        "img_id": null
      }
    ],
    "type": null
  },
  {
    "role": "paragraph",
    "segment": "Advantages and potential problems of the current structure: A structure aligned with project certainty and repetition (i.e., hierarchical for highly certain, repetitive work) can streamline communication and clarify roles, but may resist change. Conversely, a structure that embraces uncertainty through participation and open communication can better accommodate changing requirements, though it may require more coordination and encourage creativity alongside deadlines to prevent scope creep.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "A large project with high certainty and repetition probably needs a highly structured organization, whereas a small project with new techniques and a high degree of certainty needs a looser structure. Sidebar 3.2 describes the need to balance structure with creativity.",
        "img_id": null
      },
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "One way she places structure on a team is by encouraging team members to set deadlines. The overall task is broken into small subtasks, and individual team members are responsible for time estimates. The deadlines help to prevent 'scope creep,' the injection of unnecessary functionality into a product.",
        "img_id": null
      },
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "The experts in Kunde's article claim that good project management means finding a balance between structure and creativity. Left to their own devices, the software developers will focus only on functionality and creativity, disregarding deadlines and the scope of the specification.",
        "img_id": null
      }
    ],
    "type": null
  }
]

[
    {
        "role": "bullet",
        "segment": "Algorithmic methods use models that express the relationship between effort and influencing factors, typically described with equations where effort depends on system size and other cost factors.",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "Researchers have created models that express the relationship between effort and the factors that influence it.",
                "img_id": null
            },
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "Most of these models acknowledge that project size is the most influential factor in the equation by expressing effort as where S is the estimated size of the system, and a, b, and c are constants. is a vector of cost factors, through and m is an adjustment multiplier based on these factors.",
                "img_id": null
            }
        ]
    },
    {
        "role": "bullet",
        "segment": "Expert judgment relies on a manager’s experience and can be used as an initial estimate, either top-down or bottom-up, based on what is needed to build the system or its subsystems.",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "Many effort-estimation  methods  rely  on  expert  judgment. Some  are  informal  techniques, based on a manager's experience with similar projects.",
                "img_id": null
            },
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "In its simplest form, such an estimate makes an educated guess about the effort needed to build an entire system or its subsystems. The complete estimate can be computed from either a top-down or a bottom-up analysis of what is needed.",
                "img_id": null
            }
        ]
    }
]

[
    {
        "role": "paragraph",
        "segment": "Two values that can be used are (1) absolute error, the difference between the predicted value E and the actual value A, and (2) percentage (relative) error, the absolute error expressed as a percent of A. These are used by validating prediction systems: you compare the model's performance with known data in the given environment to assess accuracy, and expectations such as aiming for estimates within 10 percent of the actual value help determine acceptability.",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "We validate a prediction system in a given environment by establishing its accuracy by empirical means; that is, we compare the model's performance with known data in the given environment.",
                "img_id": null
            },
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "Many experts aim for estimates that are within 10 percent of the actual value, but Boehm's data indicate that such estimates typically occur only when the project is almost done-too late to be useful for project management.",
                "img_id": null
            }
        ],
        "type": null
    }
]

[
    {
        "role": "bullet",
