[
    {
        "role": "bullet",
        "segment": "a. The InventorySearchByID module is likely to have high cohesion (communicationally cohesive) because it operates on a data set (inventory records) and returns a data structure containing the matching records.",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "Unrelated data may be fetched together because the data are collected from the same input sensor or with a single disk access. Modules that are designed around data sets in this way are communicationally cohesive .",
                "img_id": null
            },
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "A module that hides a data representation may be informationally cohesive.",
                "img_id": null
            }
        ],
        "type": null
    },
    {
        "role": "bullet",
        "segment": "b. The ProcessPurchase module is likely to have high cohesion (procedural cohesion) because it encapsulates the sequence of actions performed to process a purchase (remove from inventory, print receipt, update log).",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "Often, functions must be performed in a certain order. For example, data must be entered before they can be checked and then manipulated. When functions are grouped together in a module to encapsulate the order of their execution, we say that the module is procedurally cohesive .",
                "img_id": null
            }
        ],
        "type": null
    },
    {
        "role": "bullet",
        "segment": "c. The FindSet module is likely to have high cohesion (functional cohesion) because it processes the user's request, determines the set of items from inventory that match the request, and formats the items into a list for display, all as part of a single functionally cohesive task.",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "Our ideal is functional cohesion , where two conditions hold: all elements essential to a single function are contained in one module, and all of that module's elements are essential to the performance of that function.",
                "img_id": null
            },
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "A functionally cohesive module performs only the function for which it is designed, and nothing else.",
                "img_id": null
            }
        ],
        "type": null
    }
]

