[
  {
    "role": "paragraph",
    "segment": "Benefits of making a separate class for each service as subclasses of a general sale item include reuse and specialization: the new subclass inherits the parent class's data and function definitions, so you only need to provide definitions that distinguish the subclass (e.g., rebinding specifics) from the parent Sale class, and you can apply polymorphism so the cost calculation can depend on whether the object is an ordinary Sale or a Bulk Sale.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "The new class, called a subclass, is said to 'inherit' the parent class's data and function definitions.",
        "img_id": null
      },
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "If we define Bulk Sale as an extension of our regular Sale class, as in Figure 6.12, then we need to provide only the definitions that distinguish Bulk Sale from its parent class, Sale .",
        "img_id": null
      },
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "Inheritance, object composition, and polymorphism are important features of an OO design that make the resulting system more useful in many ways.",
        "img_id": null
      },
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "How the cost is calculated (i.e., which method is executed and whether a discount is applied) will depend on whether the object is an ordinary Sale or a Bulk Sale .",
        "img_id": null
      }
    ],
    "type": null
  },
  {
    "role": "paragraph",
    "segment": "Possible arguments against using inheritance in this case include the fact that designs often use wrapper classes to add functionality without changing the implementations of existing classes, which can complicate the design and degrade runtime performance; and decisions should weigh trade-offs among design complexity, development time, runtime performance, fault avoidance, and ease of maintenance.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "On the downside, such  designs  often  use  a wrapper  class to  add  functionality without changing the implementations of existing classes. Although wrapper classes ease the task of adding operations to composite classes,they can complicate the design and degrade runtime performance.",
        "img_id": null
      },
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "deciding whether to adhere to the Law of Demeter involves evaluating trade-offs among design complexity, development time, runtime performance, fault avoidance, and ease of maintenance.",
        "img_id": null
      }
    ],
    "type": null
  }
]

