[
  {
    "role": "paragraph",
    "segment": "Strategy for finding a reusable component: build an inventory of components, form a matrix with the features of all past and planned projects, and rate each feature in terms of whether it has been implemented and is still needed, has been implemented but is no longer needed, or has not been implemented but is still needed; target the features that are needed and common to more than one project, and, when appropriate, redesign them to be more reusable; meet regularly (weekly for two hours) to make selections, inspect design documentation, and monitor reuse levels.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "The Council created an inventory of components and formed a matrix with the features of all past  and  planned  projects. Then, each  feature  was  rated  in  terms  of  whether  it  had  been implemented and was still needed, had been implemented but was no longer needed, or had not been implemented but was still needed.Those features that were needed and were common to more than one project were targeted for reuse. In fact, some were redesigned to make them more reusable.",
        "img_id": null
      },
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "The Council met every week for two hours to make component selections, inspect design documentation for those components already in the repository, and to monitor the levels of reuse in the department's projects.",
        "img_id": null
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "role": "paragraph",
    "segment": "Guidelines for selecting reusable components: evaluate whether the component performs the function or provides the data you need; if minor modification is required, consider whether it is less modification than building from scratch; ensure the component is well-documented so you can understand it without inspecting the implementation line by line; verify there is a complete record of the component's test and revision history to be confident that it contains no faults.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "guidelines for you. If you are a consumer for your current project, there are four key characteristics to check about the components you are about to reuse:\n\n1. Does the component perform the function or provide the data you need?\n2. If minor modification is required, is it less modification than building the component from scratch?\n3. Is the component well-documented, so you can understand it without having to verify its implementation line by line?\n4. Is there a complete record of the component's test and revision history, so you can be certain that it contains no faults?",
        "img_id": null
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "role": "paragraph",
    "segment": "Additional supporting practices: use a retrieval system that catalogs components and records information such as source, reliability (faults discovered or hours run without failure), and previous usage to aid decision making; note that the retrieval system can record unmet requests to guide component development (e.g., if several users need a date-format converter and none exists, ensure one is created).",
    "citations": [
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "The retrieval system can also record information about user requests. A volume of unmet requests of a certain type may trigger the librarian to suggest that a particular type of component be written. For example, if several users request a component to change a date from the U.S. format (11/05/98, or November 5, 1998) to the European format (05/11/98, or 5 November 1998) and no such component exists, the librarian can ensure that one is created. Similarly, if many developers search for components to draw geometric shapes but none is available, the librarian can identify good candidates and add them to the library.",
        "img_id": null
      },
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "Finally, the retrieval system can retain descriptive information about the component that aids us in deciding which components to select. Information about the component's  source  (e.g., where  it  was  purchased  or  what  project  developed  it), its reliability (the number of faults discovered or the number of hours it has run without failure), or previous usage may convince us to use one component rather than another. Sidebar 12.3 addresses how to measure the reusability of a component.",
        "img_id": null
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "role": "paragraph",
    "segment": "Takeaway: successful reuse programs emphasize organizational support and structured processes for identifying, classifying, and evaluating reusable components, with ongoing collaboration to monitor reuse levels.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "Experiences with Reuse. Several companies and organizations have been successful in reusing a significant amount of their software. In every case, committed and unconditional  management  support  has  been  a  common  characteristic  (Braun  and Prieto-Díaz 1990). Such support is necessary, since the additional costs of producer reuse must be borne by management until the savings of consumer reuse outweigh",
        "img_id": null
      }
    ]
  }
]

[
  {
    "role": "paragraph",
    "segment": "The design is a guide to the function or purpose of each component, but the programmer has great flexibility in implementing the design as code. The correspondence between design and code is essential, and testing, maintenance, and configuration management are impossible without the links established by these standards. Design by contract provides a precise interface where the contract covers mutual obligations (the preconditions), benefits (the postconditions), and consistency constraints (called invariants), and together these properties are called assertions, forming a clear basis for testing and verification.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "The design is a guide to the function or purpose of each component, but the programmer has great flexibility in implementing the design as code.",
        "img_id": null
      },
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "The correspondence between design and code is essential.",
        "img_id": null
      },
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "testing, maintenance, and configuration management are impossible without the links established by these standards.",
        "img_id": null
      },
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "In design by contract , each module has an interface specification that precisely describes what the module is supposed to do.",
        "img_id": null
      },
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "The contract covers mutual obligations (the preconditions), benefits (the postconditions), and consistency constraints (called invariants ).",
        "img_id": null
      },
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "Such specification cannot guarantee a module's correctness, but it forms a clear and consistent basis for testing and verification.",
        "img_id": null
      }
    ],
    "type": null
  }
]

[
    {
        "role": "paragraph",
        "segment": "This was consumer reuse, using components that were originally developed for other projects.",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "Reuse. There are two kinds of reuse: producer reuse , where we are creating components designed to be reused in subsequent applications, and consumer reuse , where  we  are  using  components  that  were  originally  developed  for  other  projects (Barnes and Bollinger 1991).",
                "img_id": null
            }
        ],
        "type": null
    },
    {
        "role": "paragraph",
        "segment": "Encountered problems included deciding whether to treat the reused component as black-box or clear-box, and validating that the reused module performed its required function; clear-box reuse is more common but controversial because the effort to understand and modify the component must be less than the effort to write a new equivalent component.",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "Some companies have division-wide or company-wide reuse programs, with standards for evaluating and changing components; Sidebar 7.2 describes  such  a  program  at  Lucent. The  success  of  these  programs  suggests  reuse",
                "img_id": null
            },
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "As consumers, we can either use a product whole, without modification (called black-box reuse ), or modify it to fit our particular needs (called clear-box reuse ). We perform black-box reuse often, when we use routines from mathematical libraries or build graphical interfaces from predefined sets of components.An important issue in black-box reuse is our ability to verify that a reused module performs its required function in an acceptable way. If the component is code, we want to be sure it is failure-free; if it is a test set, we want to be sure it thoroughly exercises the function for which it is intended.",
                "img_id": null
            },
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "Clear-box reuse (sometimes called white-box reuse )  is  more  common but still controversial, because the effort to understand and modify the component must be less than the effort to write a new equivalent component.",
                "img_id": null
            }
        ],
        "type": null
    },
    {
        "role": "paragraph",
        "segment": "Resolution involved revisiting and aligning the design and code: revising and rewriting as needed, and reporting and obtaining approval for design changes to maintain the links among requirements, design, and code.",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "Thus, pseudocode has acted as a framework on which to construct the code. In briefing the  code  from  the  design  in  this  way, notice  that  the  design's  organization  has changed several times. Such changes must be reported to and approved by the designers, so that the links among requirements, design, and code can be documented and maintained.",
                "img_id": null
            },
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "Revising and Rewriting, Not Patching. When writing code, as when preparing a term paper or creating a work of art, you often write a rough draft. Then you carefully revise and rewrite until you are satisfied with the result. If you find the control flow convoluted, or the decision processes hard to understand, or the unconditional branches difficult to eliminate, then it may be time to return to the design.",
                "img_id": null
            }
        ],
        "type": null
    },
    {
        "role": "paragraph",
        "segment": "Guidelines in this chapter to mitigate problems include understanding the two kinds of reuse (producer vs consumer), recognizing when black-box vs clear-box reuse is appropriate, and consulting reuse resources and domain analyses to guide implementation and evaluation.",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "Reuse. There are two kinds of reuse: producer reuse , where we are creating components designed to be reused in subsequent applications, and consumer reuse , where  we  are  using  components  that  were  originally  developed  for  other  projects (Barnes and Bollinger 1991).",
                "img_id": null
            },
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "Several advances in software engineering make reuse more feasible. One is the interest in and adoption of object-oriented development (OOD). Because OOD encourages software engineers to build a design around invariant components, many parts of the design and code can be applied to multiple systems. Another advance that encourages reuse is the increasing sophistication of tools that stretch their effects across the development life cycle. Tools that include a repository for the components developed for  one  system can encourage reuse of those components in a later system. Finally, language design can encourage component reuse. For example,Ada provides software restructuring mechanisms that support packaging, and its parameterization techniques can help to make software more general.",
                "img_id": null
            }
        ],
        "type": null
    }
]

[
    {
        "role": "paragraph",
        "segment": "Drawing from the chapter on programming standards and guidelines, here is a set of coding standards for Python, with explanations aligned to those guidelines.",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "In this chapter, we present examples from some of the popular languages, but the guidelines are generally applicable to any implementation. That is, this chapter does not teach you how to program; rather, it explains some of the software engineering practices that you should keep in mind as you write your code.",
                "img_id": null
            }
        ],
        "type": null
    },
    {
        "role": "bullet_intro",
        "segment": "Coding standards should cover the following core areas:",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "In this chapter, we have looked at several guidelines for implementing programs. We have seen that you should consider the following when you write your code:",
                "img_id": null
            }
        ],
        "type": null
    },
    {
        "role": "bullet",
        "segment": "Organizational standards and guidelines — Establish project-wide rules for code structure, style, and review processes to ensure consistency and ease of maintenance.",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "- organizational standards and guidelines",
                "img_id": null
            }
        ],
        "type": null
    },
    {
        "role": "bullet",
        "segment": "Documentation within code and external documents — Require clear in-code comments and external documentation that explain a component's organization, data, control, and function, as well as design decisions.",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "- using documentation within your programs and in external documents to explain your code's organization,data,control,and function,as well as your design decisions",
                "img_id": null
            }
        ],
        "type": null
    },
    {
        "role": "bullet",
        "segment": "Reusability and modularity — Emphasize writing reusable components and consider future projects to facilitate reuse of code and components.",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "- reusing code from other projects",
                "img_id": null
            },
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "- writing your code to make it reusable on future projects",
                "img_id": null
            }
        ],
        "type": null
    },
    {
        "role": "bullet",
        "segment": "Design-to-code alignment — Use the low-level design as the initial framework and move iteratively from design to code to preserve intended structure.",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "- using the low-level design as an initial framework, and moving in several iterations from design to code",
                "img_id": null
            }
        ],
        "type": null
    },
    {
        "role": "bullet",
        "segment": "Error handling strategy — Incorporate a systemwide approach to error handling to ensure predictability and easier maintenance.",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "- incorporating a systemwide error-handling strategy",
                "img_id": null
            }
        ],
        "type": null
    },
    {
        "role": "bullet",
        "segment": "Quality design attributes — Preserve the quality design attributes in your code to maintain clarity and adaptability over time.",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "- preserving the quality design attributes in your code",
                "img_id": null
            }
        ],
        "type": null
    },
    {
        "role": "bullet",
        "segment": "Design decisions and language choice — Let design considerations suggest an implementation language where appropriate, balancing practicality with structure.",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "- using design aspects to suggest an implementation language",
                "img_id": null
            }
        ],
        "type": null
    },
    {
        "role": "paragraph",
        "segment": "Explanation of why these standards matter:",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "Standardized documentation also helps in locating faults and making changes, because it clarifies which sections of your program perform which functions.",
                "img_id": null
            }
        ],
        "type": null
    },
    {
        "role": "paragraph",
        "segment": "Rationale for reuse, readability, and teamwork:",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "Thus, it is very important for others to be able to understand not only what you have written, but also why you have written it and how it fits in with their work.",
                "img_id": null
            },
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "In class, your work is done independently, so that your instructor can judge its quality and suggest improvements. However, in the wider world, most software is developed by teams, and a variety of jobs are required to generate a quality product.",
                "img_id": null
            }
        ],
        "type": null
    }
]

