[
  {
    "role": "paragraph",
    "segment": "Kitchenham, Pickard, and Pfleeger (1995) discuss how different research methods—surveys, case studies, and experiments—vary in generalizability and control, and they emphasize that formal experiments are usually small but more generalizable, while case studies examine typical projects and may have limited generalizability.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "There are many areas of software engineering that can be analyzed using surveys, case studies, and experiments. One key motivator for using a formal experiment rather than a case study or survey is that the results of an experiment are usually more generalizable.",
        "img_id": null
      }
    ],
    "type": null
  },
  {
    "role": "bullet_intro",
    "segment": "Potential pitfalls in case studies (per Kitchenham et al. 1995):",
    "citations": [],
    "type": null
  },
  {
    "role": "bullet",
    "segment": "Case studies usually look at a typical project, rather than trying to capture information about all possible cases; these can be thought of as 'research in the typical.'",
    "citations": [
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "Case studies usually look at a typical project, rather than trying to capture information about all possible cases; these can be thought of as 'research in the typical.'",
        "img_id": null
      }
    ],
    "type": null
  },
  {
    "role": "bullet",
    "segment": "We must take into account the limitations and lack of control when deciding whether study results apply to a new situation.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "we must take into account the limitations and lack of control when deciding whether study results apply to a new situation.",
        "img_id": null
      }
    ],
    "type": null
  },
  {
    "role": "bullet",
    "segment": "Formal experiments are carefully controlled and contrasts different values of the controlled variables, making their results generally applicable to a wider community and across several organizations.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "since formal experiments require a great deal of control, they tend to be small, involving small numbers of people or events. We can think of experiments as 'research in the small.' Case studies usually look at a typical project, rather than trying to capture information about all possible cases; these can be thought of as 'research in the typical.'And surveys try to poll what is happening broadly over large groups of projects:'research in the large.'",
        "img_id": null
      }
    ],
    "type": null
  },
  {
    "role": "paragraph",
    "segment": "If there are problems with the case study, these issues can sometimes be addressed by clarifying the study design, explicitly stating the scope and context, and using triangulation to connect findings to broader contexts, thereby improving generalizability while preserving feasibility.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "In this way, we make a safety case for the system, making explicit the ways in which our software meets performance goals for safetycritical systems.",
        "img_id": null
      }
    ],
    "type": null
  }
]

